# ── Environment Files ──
**/.env
**/.env.local
**/.env.production

# The development fallback in `utils/s3_utils.upload_file_to_s3` writes here
# when AWS is not configured. On a developer's machine that is real KYC
# material — national IDs, delivery proof — so it must never be committable.
# Outside development the same path now refuses rather than writing.
BackendAPI/uploads/

# ── Build Artifacts ──
*.apk
*.aab
*.ipa

# ── Crash Logs ──
crash_log.txt
recent_crash.txt

# ── Misc ──
.DS_Store
*.log
**/.vscode/
**/tsconfig.tsbuildinfo

# ── Scratch & Test Scripts ──
# Anchored to the directories where throwaway probes actually get dropped. These
# were previously unanchored (`test_*.py`, `scripts/`), so they matched at every
# depth and silently excluded most of BackendAPI/tests/ and every app's
# scripts/ directory — including the preflight check that `eas-build-pre-install`
# invokes, which would have failed the build with "file not found".
/test_*.py
/check_*.py
BackendAPI/test_*.py
BackendAPI/check_*.py
/scripts/
venv/

# Backup copies of config files — google-services.json.bak was tracked in HEAD
# with a live Google API key long after the original had been removed.
*.bak
*.orig
google-services.json
GoogleService-Info.plist

# SQLite artifact produced by the test suite
BackendAPI/test.db

# Google service-account keys (FCM v1 sending, Play submissions).
# Unlike google-services.json — which is client config designed to ship inside
# an APK — these are server credentials with broad access to the Firebase
# project. They must never enter the repo, and `eas credentials` offers to
# auto-detect JSON files in the project directory, which is exactly how one
# ends up here by accident.
*service-account*.json
*serviceaccount*.json
*-firebase-adminsdk-*.json
google-service-account*.json
